Title: John's Boys
Credit: written by
Author: Vicente Pérez Pourtau

!OGALLALA, NEBRASKA, 1884

INT. BIG JOHN'S OFFICE - NIGHT

A large office. BIG JOHN (late 50s) sits in a fancy chair behind a heavy desk. He is a huge man in a big brown cowboy hat and a stylish black suit.

There are two other men sitting in some chairs next to John, neither wearing a hat, both in fancy-looking cowboy suits.

MAN 1
You know, John, you're gonna have
to keep that in mind for the job,
right?

John smiles.

BIG JOHN
I've been thinking about
something...

Beat.

MAN 2
They say there's a plague coming
down from the mountains.

Big John gets up from his chair, while nodding.

BIG JOHN
Plagues can be useful. If you know
where they're heading, right?

He smiles at the men.

BIG JOHN (CONT'D)
And if it turns into trouble, you
know how I handle that kinda
trouble.

John quickly draws and empties his revolver at a hand-made target at the back of the office.

MR. B (late 30s) knocks on the door and enters the office.

Mr. B looks more like a farmer than an outlaw, wearing a humble brown suit and a simple hat.

As he enters the room, Big John approaches him.

BIG JOHN (CONT'D)
Allow me to introduce Mr. B,
gentlemen.

John shakes his hand with a smile on his face.

MR. B
Am I the first one here?

Mr. B sits on one of the chairs in front of the desk.

The two men that were talking with John leave the room.

BIG JOHN
Well, yeah, you got here a mite
early.

MR. B
Just making sure everything's all
right.

Beat.

The two of them look at each other. John smiles while Mr. B maintains a poker face.

BIG JOHN
As you can see, everything's just
fine, mister.

MR. X (late 30s) knocks on the door and goes inside the room.

He has a nice black hat and a loose black suit. He looks more like an outlaw than Mr. B.

BIG JOHN (CONT'D)
Pleased to make your acquaintance,
Mr. X.

Mr. X looks at Mr. B.

BIG JOHN (CONT'D)
Mr. X, allow me to introduce your
partner, Mr. B.

Mr. X approaches Mr. B, who doesn't get up.

They shake hands while looking at each other.

Mr. X smiles for the first time.

!KNOCK KNOCK

MR. D (O.S.)
Uh, can I come in, Mr. John?

BIG JOHN
Course, Mr. D. Come on in, you're
the last one.

MR. D (early 20s) goes inside the room. He is wearing a white shirt with a black vest over it.

MR. D
Sorry I'm late, Mr. John. Y'know,
it's awful easy to get yourself
turned around out here.

Mr. X brings a hand to his face.

MR. B
(looking at Mr. D)
You gotta be kiddin' me, John.

Mr. D looks at Big John.

BIG JOHN
Mr. D won't let you down. He may be
clumsy, gentlemen, but he's just as
sharp as they come.

Mr. D is still standing in front of the door.

MR. X
Well, uh, could you tell us what
this damn job's about now, or what?

Big John recovers his smile.

BIG JOHN
Take a seat, please, Mr. D--

Mr. D sits down in the last chair in front of the desk.

Big John sits in his chair.

BIG JOHN (CONT'D)
You three may know me by
reputation. I'm known for pullin'
off jobs that are simple and
effective. This here's a simple and
effective job. Quick and easy, like
stealin' from a church. You'll hit
the jewelry store here in Ogallala
at sundown--

Big John expertly reloads his revolver.

BIG JOHN (CONT'D)

Once you've taken the goods, the
sheriffs'll be right on your tail,
so you'd best hightail it to the
meetin' place and lay low with the
money.

MR. B
And where's that meetin' place,
John?

Mr. G enters the office.

MR. G (late 20s) has a long moustache, a brown hat and a simple brown and blue suit.

Mr. B and Mr. X keep staring at Mr. G.

Mr. D is cleaning his revolver clumsily, like he's never done it before.

BIG JOHN
Mr. G will be joinin' the three of
you on this job and leadin' you to
the meetin' place.

MR. X
You told us this was a three-man
job.

MR. B
(looking at Big John)
What're you doin', John? You
fuckin' with us?

Mr. G stares at Big John.

Big John smiles again.

BIG JOHN
Gentlemen, Mr. G's a man just like
the rest of you. He'll help you
pull off the job, and I can assure
you he's a man you can trust.

Mr. D raises his hand slightly.

MR. D
Uh, Mr. John, how much money are we
talkin' about, sir?

All four look at Big John.

BIG JOHN
Five hundred dollars a head,
gentlemen. More than any of you'll
see in two years of honest work.

As he speaks, he smiles and looks at each of their faces.

Mr. B avoids looking at Big John's face.

Mr. X smiles.

Mr. D raises a hand to his head.

Mr. G remains standing.

MR. D
Well, that's a fair bit of money,
if you ask me...

Looks at the other two men.

BIG JOHN
Tomorrow, just keep an eye on the
store. The day after's when we make
our move, gentlemen.

Mr. X gets up from the chair and heads toward the door.

Mr. B follows him, tipping his hat to Big John before leaving.

MR. D
Thank you kindly, sir.

Mr. D goes out of the office following his partners.

Mr. G makes a move to leave with them.

BIG JOHN
One moment, G. Almost forgot. This
is for you.

Big John gives a letter to Mr. G.

Mr. G looks at the paper.

Then at Big John.

BIG JOHN (CONT'D)
Walls got ears in this town, G.

MR. G
Thanks, sir.

And he goes out of the office.

Big John stands up and, in one quick movement, aims at his target and starts shooting.

His gunshots continue over black, becoming--

EXT. MAIN STREET, OGALLALA - SUNSET

The four men are running down Ogallala's Main Street.

Their suits are somewhat stained; they all have their revolvers in hand and they run, glancing back over their shoulders.

Mr. X is carrying a large bag on his back.

Some shots are coming from behind them.

They take cover behind a low fence, the four of them crouching down.

MR. B
They're right on our tail, and
since I ain't about to use D as
bait to draw 'em off, we'd best
split up till we lose 'em.

Mr. X looks at Mr. D.

MR. X
I'll take the kid with me, make
sure he don't get his brains blown
out.

Mr. D is breathing heavily, with a vacant stare; he looks exhausted.

MR. B
(looking at Mr. G)
Well, Mr. G, let's head down them
alleys over there.

Mr. G nods, as X and D head toward the other side.

Mr. B and Mr. G start running through the street, toward the alleys. The pursuers keep firing, staying out of sight.

EXT. ALLEY, OGALLALA - CONTINUOUS

Mr. B arrives at the alley first and waits for him to cross.

Mr. G starts crossing the street while the gunfire does not cease and just as he is about to reach the alley, he slips, leaving himself exposed in the middle of the street.

In that moment the gunfire stops, letting Mr. G get up and reach the dark alley.

Mr. B has seen all this from the alley, staring intently at Mr. G.

MR. G
That was a close one, I'll tell ya-
-

He approaches Mr. B's position.

Mr. B is still staring at him.

MR. G (CONT'D)
Lucky those bullets missed me.
Seems like they keep hirin' more
useless sheriffs every year.

As he finishes, they start running down the dark alley. Mr. B hasn't stopped looking at him; even after they've moved a few yards along, he aggressively pins Mr. G against the wall.

MR. B
You think I'm a damn fool, don't
ya? That what you think?!

He hits him.

MR. G
What the hell are you talkin'
about?

MR. B
You're in with the sheriff and all
them folks, ain't ya? You're
plannin' on turnin' us in, ain't
ya?

Mr. B throws him to the ground.

MR. G
I-I can explain the whole--

Mr. B shoves the barrel of his revolver into Mr. G's mouth.

!POV - FROM MR. G'S MOUTH

Mr. B looms over him, arm extended, the revolver filling the frame. His face shows nothing.

!BANG

!BLACK

EXT. ALLEY, OGALLALA - MOMENTS LATER

Mr. X and Mr. D reach the entrance to the alley; Mr. X is firing outwards as they enter. He still has the bag with him.

Upon arriving, Mr. D makes a move as if to slip, then rights himself and adjusts his hat.

Mr. G's dead body lies on the ground, with the gaping wound in his mouth still bleeding.

Mr. B is leaning against the far end of the alley, keeping watch to ensure no one is coming.

!POV - FROM MR. G'S MOUTH

MR. X
What the hell happened here?

Mr. X and Mr. D are both looking at the body.

Mr. B approaches them.

MR. B
Those bastards got him while we
were makin' our getaway.

Mr. X looks away from the dead body.

Mr. D continues to keep his gaze fixed on Mr. G's wound.

Beat.

!BACK TO SCENE

Immediately, Mr. D fixes his gaze on Mr. B.

MR. D
What do we do with him?

MR. B
What d'you mean, what do we do with
him? Give him mouth-to-mouth or
what?

Mr. X goes to check that no one is coming from one end of the alley.

Mr. D gives him a long, cold look.

MR. D
We can't just leave him here.
Besides, he was a friend of John's.

!POV - FROM MR. G'S MOUTH

MR. B
Oh, come on, kid. This kinda thing
happens on jobs like these. Now
let's get our asses to the meetin'
point, goddammit.

Mr. X stands next to Mr. D.

MR. X
Mr. G was the only one who knew
where it was.

Mr. B takes a step forward, towards Mr. X and Mr. D.

!BACK TO SCENE

MR. B
You mean to tell me none of you
thought to ask him where we were
supposed to meet the boss?

Both Mr. D and Mr. X look at each other.

MR. X
Don't reckon you know much either,
Mr. B.

Some shots can be heard from the background.

Mr. B and Mr. D look in the direction of the noise.

MR. B
Enough jawin'. We gotta find us a
place to lay low.

MR. D
But we're takin' G along.

Mr. B looks at Mr. D.

MR. X
He's one of John's, B. He comes
with us.

Mr. X starts grabbing the corpse, without looking at it directly.

MR. X (CONT'D)
Give me a hand here, kid.

Mr. D lifts the body from the other end.

MR. B
All right, gentlemen, let's go find
us a hole to crawl into.

Mr. B starts running through the alley.

Mr. X and Mr. D follow him with the body. Mr. D keeps staring at Mr. B as he is running.

!FADE TO BLACK

INT. BARN - SUNSET

The barn is divided into two floors. The second floor is half the size of the first. There is a bunch of square hay bales and haystacks on both floors. Also there are some dirty windows along the walls.

The main door looks locked. But there's a regular door by one side of the big one. Mr. B kicks it open and enters.

Mr. X and Mr. D follow, carrying the corpse between them.

Mr. B goes to the back of the barn, revolver raised and looking behind the haystacks.

MR. B
(looking around the walls)
Well, I've slept in worse. This'll
do.

They put Mr. G on the floor.

MR. D
Well, now what?

He sits next to the body. Mr. X never once looks at the body. He goes to the far end of the barn.

MR. D (CONT'D)
(looking closely at Mr. G's head)
Well, first thing, we gotta get
word to John.

MR. B
Hold your horses. Don't forget, we
got a whole town out there lookin'
for us.

Mr. X laughs.

Mr. B turns to Mr. X, approaching him. Mr. X is cleaning his revolver with a black cloth.

MR. B (CONT'D)
You think this is funny? Sure you
do. With all your mysterious
horseshit, this is just a walk in
the park for you, ain't it?

MR. X
(still looking at his weapon)
Easy there, big man. I was just
figurin' how to get us outta here,
and all I could see was our skulls
bustin' open from the sheriff's
lead.

Horses whinny outside.

Mr. B shakes his head and turns away, with one hand on his head.

MR. D
(looking at both of them)
Well, reckon we're all agreed we
gotta sit tight till them folks out
there clear off, right?

MR. X
Yeah. Reckon.

MR. B
Ain't got much choice, do we?

Mr. D stops staring at the corpse and looks at Mr. B.

Mr. X glances at the body over his hands, but as soon as he sees it, he closes his eyes and tilts his head back.

CLOSE ON the dirty window, night is approaching; some shouts can be heard from the street.

Through it, in TIME-LAPSE, the sky darkens from orange to deep blue.

INT. BARN - NIGHT

The three of them are at different corners. Mr. B is eating something from a tin; it doesn't look particularly appetizing, but he's eating it with quite a bit of relish. Mr. D is cleaning his boots with a small cloth. Mr. X is leaning against a haystack, with his legs stretched out and his hat covering his eyes, resting.

There are some hurricane lanterns around the barn.

Some shouts can be heard from outside.

MR. D
Lord, don't they ever quit? That's
five times they've passed down that
damn street. You'd think by now
it'd dawn on 'em we're hidin'.

As Mr. B eats, he looks toward Mr. G's corpse, still in the middle of the barn.

MR. B
(getting up)
Reckon I'll go through his pockets,
see if there's anything sayin'
where we're s'posed to meet up with
the boss.

Mr. D looks up, with the cloth still in his hand.

MR. D
(getting up while shaking his head and smiling at Mr. B)
Sit tight, I got it.

MR. B
(smiling at Mr. D)
I don't want you havin' nightmares,
kid, pawin' through a dead man's
pockets.

MR. D
I'm goin', and that's the end of
it.

He smiles one last time at Mr. B.

Pause.

MR. B
All right, big boy.

Mr. B stops and leans against a nearby wall.

Mr. X puts on his hat and stands up, approaching the rest.

Mr. D leans over the corpse and begins emptying its pockets; he finds a watch, a handkerchief, and... he pulls a small letter from one of the jacket pockets.

Mr. X narrows his eyes, while Mr. B places a hand over his mouth, in a thoughtful manner.

Mr. D reads the letter for himself.

MR. X
Whatcha got there, D?

Mr. D's breathing slows.

MR. D
It says, "Keep an eye on him".

A low musical STING

He looks up at Mr. B, who keeps his hands over his mouth.

Mr. D reads it again.

MR. D (CONT'D)
I...I don't understand.

Mr. X keeps looking at the letter.

MR. X
(speaking loudly)
There's nothing to understand. We
got bigger troubles than readin'
some scrap of paper.

Mr. X goes away, back to his spot at the back of the barn.

Mr. D and Mr. B look at each other one last time.

MR. B
(loud enough for Mr. X to hear)
Looks like somebody didn't take too
kindly to our pal's letter, huh?

Mr. D smiles at him.

MR. D
That's for sure.

Mr. B starts climbing to the second floor.

MR. B
Gonna rest my eyes a spell, D. Good
night.

Mr. D looks once again at the wound in Mr. G's mouth.

MR. D
Sure, good night, Mr. B.

Slow PUSH IN on Mr. G's wound.

Uneasy, tense music builds.

!CUT TO BLACK

INT. BARN - LATER

Mr. D wakes up from his nap abruptly, as if rising from a nightmare. He looks around, taking note of his current location. Once settled, he heads over to look out the window near the door.

No sound from outside can be heard.

He runs through the barn toward Mr. X.

MR. D
Hey! Mr. X, rouse yourself!

Mr. X is sleeping with his hat over his eyes and his legs stretched out in his corner of the barn. He has taken off his suit jacket and left it on a hay bale.

As soon as he hears Mr. D's voice, he gives a slight start, pushing his hat up.

MR. D (CONT'D)
(looking at the second floor)
Psst, hey, B, wake up!

MR. B (O.S.)
Huh? What's wrong, boy?

MR. D
It's gone quiet out there. Ain't
heard a soul. We gotta move now.

MR. B (O.S.)
Fine. And where exactly we s'posed
to go? We don't know where John's
meetin' place is.

MR. X
We could send him a message.

Mr. D looks at him. Mr. X is getting up, putting on his jacket.

MR. B (O.S.)
And who's s'posed to get it to
John, huh?

MR. X
I'll find me a saloon that's open
and pay some kid to run it. I'll
wait for word back there myself.

Mr. B gets up and quickly heads downstairs, putting on his hat and jacket as he descends.

MR. B
Sure, just stroll into a saloon.
Never mind half the town's huntin'
us.

Mr. X positions himself in front of Mr. B, looking him in the eyes. We can see Mr. D at the back of the room, looking at both of them.

Both Mr. X and Mr. B look each other in the eye.

Beat.

MR. X
I'm open to better ideas. Besides,
this way I can get me a drink.

Beat.

MR. B
Well, ain't you the clever one. Go
on, then.
But if things turn ugly and you
sell us out, I'll be sure to open
that clever skull of yours myself.

He smiles as he finishes.

Mr. B steps aside, and Mr. X begins walking toward the door. Mr. D stays a bit apart from the two, observing everything.

MR. D
Beggin' your pardon, Mr. X, but how
you gonna know where to send him?

MR. X
(not meeting his eye)
John told me before the job. I know
where his letters go.

As Mr. X leaves, we see Mr. D, his gaze fixed firmly on the door.

The tense music returns.

!BLACKOUT

INT. BARN - LATER

Mr. B now lies in Mr. X's spot, at the back of the barn. He's lying down in the same way Mr. X was sleeping before, with his hat over his eyes. He whistles a tune. He's good at it. Mr. D is not far away from him, sitting down on the floor, writing something in a small brown notebook.

MR. B
(without lifting his hat from his eyes)
What're you writin', kid?

Mr. D barely looks at him, he keeps writing.

MR. D
Ain't none of your business.

MR. B
Easy, kid, I was only askin'.

He sets his hat right.

MR. B (CONT'D)
Where's home for you, boy?

Mr. D abruptly closes the notebook and finally looks at Mr. B.

MR. D
You know we ain't s'posed to share
nothin' personal amongst ourselves.

MR. B
Well, son, there's a heap of things
in this life we ain't s'posed to
do. You figure it's right to rob a
store and get clean away with it?
But we do it anyhow, 'cause that
money's gonna end up in the same
places one way or another, whether
we take it or not. You think that
jeweler ain't gonna sit down with
his cronies at the card table and
lose it all anyhow? So, to spare
him feelin' low about bein' a card
fiend or a drunkard, or whatever he
is, I rob him first, and he just
figures he had a run of bad luck.
I'll do the gamblin' and drinkin'
for him.

Beat.

MR. D
(staring at Mr. B)
Who are you, Mr. B?

Mr. B starts getting up, stretching his arms and legs.

MR. B
(approaching Mr. D)
Nobody, "Mister D". Just a man
tryin' to get by in this life. And
I ain't gonna feed you that "I'm a
survivor" horseshit. I've done
things I oughta hang for, and
things I'm proud of. That's life.
I'm just lookin' for my place in
all of it.

He stops in front of Mr. D.

MR. D
What kind of things do you regret
doing, sir?

MR. B
(staring into nothingness)
Did a job once. Truth is, it
weren't nothin' like what we're
doin' now. We went into a mine to
rob some miners' camp.
It was my first job, and I felt
like a real cowboy, you know? My
pa's revolver on my hip, headin'
down into that mine with the rest
of the boys. But the end's a blur.
All that gunfire...

Mr. B turns around and starts walking through the barn.

He puts his hand to his head.

Now to his eyes. He scratches a bit and sniffles.

Mr. D is observing him without looking away from his position.

MR. D
I'm from Clay County. Missouri.

Mr. B turns his head to Mr. D.

MR. B
(smiling)
Fort Worth. Texas.

Mr. B settles back in, looking toward the window from his spot.

MR. D
Well, let's get some rest till Mr.
X comes back.

MR. B
(stroking his chin)
Yeah...X.

MR. D
What about X?

MR. B
(whispering a bit)
Think on it. He claims John told
him where to send word, before the
job. You ever see the two of 'em
off alone? 'Cause I was the first
one there, and 'Rest-In-Peace' G
rolled in last--

As he says the name of Mr. G, Mr. D cannot help but look back at the corpse, and immediately at Mr. B again.

MR. B (CONT'D)
--There weren't never a moment they
were alone.

MR. D
So... maybe it was X that G was
'keepin' an eye on', right?

MR. B
Ain't it clear as day?

MR. D
Well, I s'pose you're right on this
one.

MR. B
(nodding rapidly, almost as if he has a nervous tic)
Course I'm right.

Mr. D gets up and approaches him.

MR. D
So, what've you got in mind?

Mr. B also gets up.

MR. B
Well, nothin' too fancy...

INT. BARN - A FEW MINUTES LATER

From near the door, we watch as it swings open abruptly. All lanterns are out. The barn is dark.

Mr. X goes inside the barn, tucking his revolver into his belt as he enters, and making sure no one sees him go in.

MR. X
(whispering)
Boys?

The door closes behind him.

!POV - FROM MR. X

His eyes sweep the hayloft, then the shadows at the far end of the barn, hunting for the slightest movement.

Beat.

!BACK TO SCENE

The camera PULLS BACK and begins to slowly circle around Mr. X, coming to a stop in front of him, capturing his full figure. He stands motionless, his attention fixed on the barn.

Behind him, crouched down behind some hay bales, two men keep watch: Mr. D and Mr. B.

Mr. B and Mr. D are slowly positioning themselves behind him. They have their revolvers pointed at Mr. X.

MR. B
Hold it right there, X.

Without turning his body, Mr. X turns his head to look toward the two men.

MR. X
(raising his arms)
All right, all right. Damn.

MR. B
Here's what happens now, X. You
throw that six-gun of yours my way,
nice and slow, and you explain just
who the hell you real--

MR. X
John's coming tomorrow morning.

Mr. B relaxes his arm a little, looking over his revolver. Mr. D looks at Mr. B.

MR. B
The hell you talkin' about?

Mr. X turns around and throws his revolver to the ground.

MR. X
John answered. Sunup, he's here,
gentlemen.

Mr. B scratches his head with his revolver, then points it at Mr. X again, with less firmness than before.

Mr. D approaches Mr. X and picks up his revolver from the floor.

MR. B
(still aiming at Mr. X)
Well done, Mr. X. Real fine work.
Now sit down and tell us just who
in God's name you are.

Mr. X walks toward one end of the barn, where there are some piles of straw, and sits down. As he sits, he lets out a heavy breath. Mr. D and Mr. B follow him the entire time, keeping their revolvers trained on him. Upon reaching the pile of straw, they come to a halt, still aiming their revolvers at him.

Mr. D appears to be trembling slightly and not aiming quite correctly, as if he's never been in a spot like this.

Mr. X pulls a small flask from his jacket and takes a good swig.

MR. X
This ain’t my first time workin’
for Big John. A few months past, he
gave me and some other boys a job
back East.

!QUICK FLASHES

- Mr. X and some other cowboys going inside a saloon, pointing their guns at everyone inside. They all have their faces covered with bandanas.

MR. X (V.O.)
But then it all went bad. Real bad.
Quicker'n you'd believe.

- A crossfire between bandits

- People getting shot, all the blood exploding like paint all over the saloon.

- Mr. X looking at a door at the back of the saloon.

!END FLASHES

MR. X
I had cause to send him word then,
so I know where it goes.

Mr. B stares at Mr. X. Still aiming at him. Then Mr. B lets out a short laugh. Mr. X doesn't laugh back.

MR. B
Didn't I say it, D? This lyin'
bastard's been stringin' us along
from the start.

MR. X
Oh, I'm the liar here, am I? You
were the only one with G when he
died. You sure you didn't do him in
yourself, you crazy son of a bitch?

The moment Mr. X mentions Mr. G, we see Mr. D looking at Mr. B for a second. He stops pointing at Mr. X.

Mr. B laughs in a somewhat maniacal way.

MR. B
(eyes still on Mr. X)
Aw, come on, D. For God's sake,
don't believe this lunatic.

Suddenly, we see Mr. D pull Mr. X's revolver from his belt and raise both guns toward Mr. X and Mr. B. Mr. D's doing this as he looks at both of them.

Beat.

We can hear some noise from outside, mainly horses. Quite close by.

SHERIFF (O.S.)
(shouting)
Evenin', gentlemen! This here's the
sheriff.

The three outlaws freeze at the sheriff's voice. They glance at the door, then split up, searching for a place to hide.

SHERIFF (O.S.) (CONT'D)
We know you're still skulkin'
'round here like bugs under a rock.
Just stopped by to pay my respects.

The three of them are hidden on the ground floor of the barn. On the sides, we can see Mr. B and Mr. D, who are able to see each other. Mr. X, meanwhile, is located at the back of the barn.

SHERIFF (O.S.) (CONT'D)
And I swear, if any man kills you
before I do, I'll take it personal.

SHERIFF'S DEPUTY (O.S.)
(shouting)
We know BILLY BRIGHTON's ridin'
with you.

A low musical STING.

Upon hearing that name, Mr. B can't help looking down; his breathing quickens and he mutters under his breath. Mr. D has been watching him since the name was spoken. We see a close- up of Mr. D's poker face, staring fixedly at Mr. B.

A smack to the back of the head is heard from outside.

SHERIFF (O.S.)
You're about the dumbest son of a
bitch ever born. No names, you damn
fool.

We return to Mr. D's face. All the surrounding noise falls silent, as if we were underwater.

MR. X
(muffled, distant)
I think they've cleared off.

MR. X (CONT'D)
Any of you catch what the sheriff
was goin' on about?

MR. B
Didn't catch it.

The muffled sound vanishes, just as Mr. D's face turns toward them.

MR. D
Couldn't make it out.

!BLACKOUT

INT. BARN - SUNRISE

The first rays of sunlight stream in through the window. When the pursuers left, Mr. B and Mr. X fell asleep in their hiding places, unlike Mr. D.

When Mr. B wakes up, he finds himself tied to a post, back- to-back with Mr. X. He tries to strain against the rope, thereby waking Mr. X as well.

Then Mr. B sees Mr. D, who has a revolver in his hand.

MR. B
Have you lost your damn mind, you
spoiled brat?

MR. D
I want the truth, and I ain't
backin' down till I get it.

Mr. X just looks at the floor.

MR. B
You know what I think, D? You're a
kid who ain't never been told no,
and now you wanna play cowboys with
the big boys.

MR. X
Leave the kid be.

Mr. D keeps his eyes on Mr. B; he doesn't pay much attention to Mr. X.

MR. B
I'll remind you that 'kid' has got
us tied up, you sick bastards.
You're both sick in the head.

Mr. X watches Mr. D's trembling grip on the gun.

MR. X
Well... reckon I ain't been
altogether straight with you.

Mr. D looks at Mr. X closely.

MR. X (CONT'D)
When I worked for Big John...

!INTERCUT - FLASHBACK (SALOON) / BARN (PRESENT)

- We see the same scene as before: the men entering the saloon.

- A crossfire

MR. X (V.O.)
...at the end of the job, they had
us cornered.

- BARN: Behind his back, Mr. B's fingers work at the rope

- We see a group of men taking cover behind the bar while being fired upon.

- We see Mr. X standing apart from the group, looking toward the door we saw in the previous flashback. He glances at his companions and then back at the door.

MR. X (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(with a somewhat broken voice)
I ran... and I didn't look back.
Left 'em all.

- BARN: Mr. B's hands slip free. Mr. D doesn't notice.

- We see Mr. X running down the street, bearing a few wounds. In the background, we see the saloon explode.

!END INTERCUT

MR. X
(looking at the floor)
Since then, I can't stop seein'
their families...Mas and pas
waitin' on sons that ain't never
comin' home. They wasn't no older'n
you, D--

Mr. B has worked himself free. In a swift motion, he lunges at Mr. D, striking him in the face and disarming him. He positions himself over him, aiming his revolver at him. Putting the revolver into his mouth.

CLOSE ON the struggle.

MR. B
(laughing, wild-eyed)
Who the hell -- who the HELL do you
think you are?! You think this gets
you anywhere!? Spoiled little
thing... tyin' knots like his mama
taught him. You think a knot holds
a man like me? Nothin' holds me.
Nothin'. And when I'm loose... oh,
boy. When I'm loose--

As Mr. B is finishing speaking, we see Mr. X. He has already untied himself.

He takes a glance toward the barn door. A one-second glimpse of the saloon door. Then we see a shot of Mr. X's face. Decided.

And then... Mr. X lunges at Mr. B.

MR. B (CONT'D)
What the hell!

Mr. X shoves Mr. B away from Mr. D. But as soon as Mr. B falls, he grabs Mr. X by the neck and throws him to the ground, climbing on top of him.

Then Mr. B hits him.

And again.

And again.

Mr. B gets up, wiping his face and some saliva away. Mr. X is on the ground, his face a raw, bloody mess.

MR. B (CONT'D)
(spitting as he talks)
Well, ain't that a surprise. I
respected you some, X. Then you
went and proved me wrong, piece by
piece!

Mr. X and Mr. D lie on the floor, listening to  Mr. B.

MR. B (CONT'D)
You ain't no man, X. You’re gonna
let the kid die, same as them poor
dumb bastards you left behind.

He beats Mr. X again.

MR. B (CONT'D)
And I ain’t even gonna give you the
privilege of seein’ where I put the
bullet in him.

Mr. B shoves the barrel of his revolver into Mr. X's mouth.

!POV - FROM MR. X'S MOUTH

Mr. B looms over him, arm extended, the revolver filling the frame. His face shows nothing.

!BANG

!BLACK

!BACK TO SCENE

Mr. B turns his gaze toward the door. Horses can be heard approaching the barn.

The door opens and Big John enters the barn. Alone. He begins to walk toward Mr. B and Mr. D with his hands behind his back; Mr. D is still on the ground, his face covered in blood, while Mr. B still holds his revolver in his hand.

BIG JOHN
(looking down at Mr. X's body)
Looks like you boys have been busy.

We see Mr. D's face. He is breathing rapidly, practically hyperventilating.

BIG JOHN (CONT'D)
(giving a smile to Mr. B)
So... where's my money, boys?

Mr. B lets out a small laugh.

MR. B
It's in the back, John. In a sack.

BIG JOHN
Good. Real good, gentlemen.

Big John walks at a brisk pace; he is truly eager to see the money. As he passes by the corpses of Mr. G and Mr. X, he doesn't even glance at their faces.

Before Big John reaches the money, Mr. B glances at the letter next to Mr. G: "Keep an eye on him." He begins aiming his revolver at Big John. From behind.

He closes one eye, eases his finger onto the trigger, and...

Mr. D has stood up and lunges at Mr. B, disarming him and grabbing Mr. B's revolver from his hand. He grabs Mr. X's revolver from the floor.

Mr. D pushes Mr. B and aims at both him and Big John.

MR. D
(trembling, a flicker of madness in his eyes)
Don't -- don't nobody move! I swear
to God, I'll--nobody MOVE, damn
your hides!

BIG JOHN
Would you look at the kid! He's got
some balls on h--

MR. D
Shut your fuckin' mouth, John!

Big John stares at the young Mr. D.

Mr. B laughs on the ground. Mr. D kicks him in the face.

MR. D (CONT'D)
I know who he is, John. I want the
full reward on this madman's head.

He points at Mr. B.

MR. D (CONT'D)
And don't think I won't gun you
both down and light out with the
money. I won't lose a wink of
sleep.

Big John starts to laugh.

A real laugh.

BIG JOHN
HAHAHA, that was, HAHAHA

MR. D
What's wrong with you, old man?

BIG JOHN
(slow, mocking applause)
No, really, that was mighty fine.
Real stirrin'. But you honestly
figure I'd walk into this
godforsaken barn, with you crazy
sons of bitches, without havin' the
whole place ringed with my armed
men, just waitin' on the word to
come in?

Mr. D's face falls. His arms sag, revolvers dipping.

MR. D
Wh-what?

With a swift movement, Big John draws his revolver and, firing from the hip, empties the entire cylinder toward Mr. D.

Big John tucks his revolver back into his belt.

BIG JOHN
Now, gentlemen, if you please.

Glass can be heard breaking.

SHERIFF
Don't move!

A dozen men in suits enter the barn, all aiming at Mr. B.

Big John stands at the back, staring at Mr. B.

Mr. B raises his hands and begins to kneel down.

SHERIFF'S DEPUTY
Get down, you crazy bastard!

One of the men charges at Mr. B and knocks him to the ground.

A woman enters the barn with a large camera on a tripod.

BIG JOHN
(looking at the camera)
Great!

Big John crouches down and grabs the bag containing the money, then approaches the detainee, Mr. B. Once he is by his side, he places the bag next to Mr. B's face.

BIG JOHN (CONT'D)
Give us a pretty smile for the
photograph, Billy!

SLOW MOTION. The colors drain, muted and pale.

Mr. B gives the camera a big smile.

MR. B (V.O.)
Did a job once. Went into a mine,
my pa's revolver on my hip... felt
like a real cowboy. But the end's a
blur. All that gunfire, and then--

CLICK. The camera takes the photo.

FREEZE FRAME. The image becomes a sepia photograph.

!BLACKOUT

INT. SALOON - EVENING

We see a modest saloon, with a few customers sitting in the back, a couple of bartenders, and Big John sitting at the bar. He downs a glass of whiskey in one gulp.

!SUPER: A FEW WEEKS EARLIER...

We stay on Big John for the entire scene. Behind him, out of frame, two bartenders talk in low voices. Their words slowly become clear.

BARTENDER #1 (O.S.)
Oh yeah? Who is he, then?

BARTENDER #2 (O.S.)
Well, now, seein' as this country's
like somethin' out of a fever
dream, I could name you five men in
America more dangerous than Doc
Holliday.

Big John is playing with his glass, as if he is analyzing it.

BARTENDER #1 (O.S.)
Five, you say? Go on, I'm
listenin'.

BARTENDER #2 (O.S.)
Well... there's the one and only
Jesse James--

BARTENDER #1 (O.S.)
Dead.

Big John smiles, still looking at the glass.

BARTENDER #2 (O.S.)
Cole Younger--

BARTENDER #1 (O.S.)
Prison.

BARTENDER #2 (O.S.)
John Wesley Hard--

BARTENDER #1 (O.S.)
Prison.

BARTENDER #2 (O.S.)
Fine, fine, I take your meaning.
But what about that lunatic who
killed off his own gang on a job?

Big John stops playing with the glass and frowns.

BARTENDER #1 (O.S.)
That Billy Brighton fella?

BARTENDER #2 (O.S.)
Yeah... they say there's a price on
that fella's head, twenty thousand
dollars.

Big John turns his head towards the bartenders.

BARTENDER #1 (O.S.)
Good God. That's crazy.

BIG JOHN
Good evenin', gentlemen. Beg
pardon, but I chanced to overhear
you. Might either of you know the
location of that Billy Brighton?

BARTENDER #2 (O.S.)
Can't rightly say, sir. After he
done in his gang, he took to the
mountains. Nobody's seen hide nor
hair of him since. If they had, I'd
be out there claimin' that money.

BIG JOHN
What about his family? Has he any
folks?

Big John puts on a rather serious expression, staring fixedly at the bartenders.

Beat.

BARTENDER #1 (O.S.)
Well, mister, his pa lives down in
Texas. Doubt he knows anything, and
if he does, he won't talk. That old
man just wants all this to be over
and for his boy to have a decent
life.

BARTENDER #2 (O.S.)
But suit yourself, sir.

BIG JOHN
Where'd you say his father's place
is?

!KNOCK KNOCK KNOCK

Slow PUSH IN on Big John.

!KNOCK KNOCK KNOCK

!BLACKOUT

EXT. BRIGHTON RANCH - FRONT DOOR - DAY

The door of the house opens.

We are in a run-down ranch house. Big John is in the doorway, dressed in a more sophisticated suit than on previous occasions.

The man inside cracks the door just enough to show his face and one hand.

BILLY'S FATHER
What're you after, mister?

BIG JOHN
(smiling)
Beg pardon. I'm here to see Mister
Brighton.

BILLY'S FATHER
(starts to shut the door)
That's me. Now, if you ain't here
with money for me or you ain't a
pretty gal, we're done here.

Big John grabs the door.

BIG JOHN
Hold on, sir, you haven't let me
introduce myself. I'll confess I
ain't a pretty woman, but money,
that I can offer.

Billy's father stares at him.

BILLY'S FATHER
Talk.

Big John lets go of the door, and Billy's father opens it all the way. We see a man in his sixties, with a long beard and a pair of farmer's overalls that have a few stains on them.

BIG JOHN
Allow me. John's the name. I'm a
man of some standin' in Nebraska,
sir, known for doin' right by the
folks in my town. I've traveled a
long way, takin' an interest in
your boy Billy's troubles. I
believe I can help.

As soon as the father hears the name Billy, he lowers his gaze and his shoulders sag.

BILLY'S FATHER
(voice breaking)
I swear before God, mister... if
this is a joke... if you mean to
give up my boy--

BIG JOHN
(looking him in the eye)
Listen here, sir. Providence has
smiled on your family, it truly
has. It's my callin' in life,
takin' young men out of trouble
like your boy, settin' 'em to work,
and givin' 'em a fresh start.

Billy's father looks at him, teary-eyed.

BIG JOHN (CONT'D)
You have my solemn word, sir. As
God is my witness.

We see the face of Billy's father, on the verge of breaking down in tears.

!BLACKOUT

MAN 1 (O.S.)
So when you talked to him, did he
go along with it easy?

BIG JOHN (O.S.)
Sure. Truth is, he just wanted to
make his old man happy too.

INT. BIG JOHN'S OFFICE - NIGHT

We see the same office as at the beginning. Big John is sitting and talking with the two men.

MAN 2
It's all fallin' your way so far,
John, I'll give you that. But we're
in Nebraska. To collect that Texas
reward, you gotta catch him in the
act, right here--

MAN 1
My boys'll make it look real.

MAN 2
But nobody touches Brighton, and
nobody touches your man--

MAN 1
You know, John, you're gonna have
to keep that in mind for the job,
right?

SLOW PUSH IN on BIG JOHN's face.

Big John smiles.

BIG JOHN
I've been thinking about
something...

> CUT TO:

!TITLE: BIG JOHN'S BOYS
